-- Indexes that cannot discriminate, dropped. Not "rarely used" — STRUCTURALLY
-- incapable of excluding a row, so no future query and no amount of seeding can
-- make them earn their maintenance. Each is proven below, not estimated.
--
-- Context (MEASURED 2026-08-03, mid-ingest, whole-database-life counters):
--     heap                            146 GB
--     indexes                         178 GB   <- more index than data
--       >=100 lifetime scans          114 GB across   928 indexes
--       <100 scans ever                41 GB across 1,249 indexes
--       never scanned                  23 GB across 1,726 indexes
-- Every one of those is maintained on every INSERT and COPY. These three are the
-- subset that can be retired on proof rather than on usage statistics, which a
-- reseed resets to zero anyway.
--
-- SCOPE, deliberately small. Most of that 178 GB was a SYMPTOM of the partition
-- layout, not of the index set: under RANGE(hilbert_index) one leaf held 60.67% of
-- physicalities, so its indexes were single enormous structures taking random
-- writes with no cache locality. HASH(id) puts each at ~1/64th (measured on the
-- 2026-08-04 foundation seed: worst partition 1.591%, 57,502-68,234 rows against a
-- 67,012 average), which fits cache and collapses most of that insert cost on its
-- own. Refining the rest of the index set is a later exercise against a seeded box
-- with real read traffic. Only indexes that cannot discriminate under ANY
-- partitioning are retired here.
--
-- 1. entities_tier_brin — BRIN (tier) on a LIST(tier)-partitioned table.
--    A BRIN stores min/max per page range. Inside any leaf, tier is a CONSTANT:
--        SELECT count(DISTINCT tier) FROM entities_t2  ->  1
--    so every range summarises to min = max and no range can ever be excluded.
--    Partition pruning already does the whole job:
--        EXPLAIN SELECT count(*) FROM entities WHERE tier = 2
--          -> prunes to entities_t2_h*, then Parallel Seq Scan, BRIN unconsulted
--    The comment it carried ("tier banding for ingest validation and
--    tier-distribution reads at scale") describes work the partition key does.
--
-- 2. entities_tier_type_brin — BRIN (tier, type_id) on the same table. Leading
--    column constant per leaf, so it degenerates to a BRIN on type_id, which
--    entities_type_btree already indexes with far better selectivity. The prior
--    drop_entities_tier_type_btree module kept these two on the grounds they were
--    "cheap"; cheap is not the same as capable.
--
-- 3. physicalities_type_btree — btree (type) where the column is near-constant.
--    PhysicalityType declares two values (Content = 1, BuildingBlock = 2) and the
--    seeded corpus holds exactly one:
--        SELECT type, count(*) FROM physicalities GROUP BY type
--          -> type 1: 4,327,399 rows   (single group)
--    A btree over one distinct value across millions of rows returns the whole
--    table and is never chosen. The predicates that matter are already carried by
--    the partial indexes, which encode `type = 1` in their own WHERE clauses.
--
-- EXPLICITLY KEPT: physicalities_constituents_gin. It is the most expensive index
-- in the schema by write cost — one entry per constituent, and chess trajectories
-- average 137, so it takes roughly 96% of index-entry writes on this table — and
-- it is also the one that must not be touched.
--
-- It is the index the READ MODEL stands on. The core operation composes a point
-- locally from content (a hash, no database round-trip at all) and then asks which
-- trajectories CONTAIN that point: `constituent_ids(trajectory) @> ARRAY[point]`.
-- Precedes, follows, contains, part-of and co-occurrence are all that same probe
-- read at different offsets. Without the GIN it is a sequential scan of every
-- trajectory in the substrate, which is not a slower plan but a different system.
--
-- Do NOT retire it on an idx_scan of zero. Those counters are cumulative since the
-- last stats reset, and the 2026-08-03 snapshot above was taken MID-INGEST, when
-- the write path was saturating the box and nothing was querying. A low scan count
-- in that window measures what the seed was doing, not what the index is for — and
-- a reseed zeroes the counters outright. The honest open question is narrower: the
-- index predicate is `type = 1 AND trajectory IS NOT NULL`, which is every
-- trajectory physicality, while the recall query filters to Sentence entities.
-- Entity type lives on `entities`, not here, so the predicate cannot be narrowed
-- without denormalising. That is a design question about how recall reaches
-- trajectories, and it is not answered by deleting the index.
DO $do$
DECLARE
    idx text;
BEGIN
    FOREACH idx IN ARRAY ARRAY[
        'entities_tier_brin',
        'entities_tier_type_brin',
        'physicalities_type_btree'
    ] LOOP
        BEGIN
            EXECUTE format('ALTER EXTENSION %I DROP INDEX @extschema@.%I',
                           'laplace_substrate', idx);
        EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN
            NULL;
        END;
        EXECUTE format('DROP INDEX IF EXISTS @extschema@.%I', idx);
    END LOOP;
END $do$;
